      "type": "paragraph",
      "num": "§ 11",
      "original": "It was a thousand pities that the woman who could write like that, whose mind was tuned to nature and reflection, should have been forced to anger and bitterness. But how could she have helped herself? I asked, imagining the sneers and the laughter, the adulation of the toadies, the scepticism of the professional poet. She must have shut herself up in a room in the country to write, and been torn asunder by bitterness and scruples perhaps, though her husband was of the kindest, and their married life perfection. She \"must have\", I say, because when one comes to seek out the facts about Lady Winchilsea, one finds, as usual, that almost nothing is known about her. She suffered terribly from melancholy, which we can explain at least to some extent when we find her telling us how in the grip of it she would imagine:",
      "zh": "一个能够写出这等好诗、心智天生契合自然与沉思的女人，竟被逼得满怀愤怒与苦涩，这真是万分可惜。可是她又有什么办法呢？我暗自发问，一面想象着那些冷嘲热讽、哄堂讪笑、趋炎附势之徒的阿谀奉承，以及职业诗人的狐疑挑剔。想来她必定是把自己关在乡间宅邸的某个房间里写作，内心被苦涩与重重顾虑撕扯得支离破碎——尽管她的丈夫再仁慈体贴不过，他们的婚姻生活也堪称美满无瑕。我之所以说「必定是」，是因为一旦人们着手去搜寻关于温奇尔西夫人的生平事实，便会像往常一样发现，关于她几乎一无所知。她饱受忧郁症的剧烈折磨，而当我们读到她自述在忧郁症发作时如何浮想联翩，便至少能在一定程度上解释这种病症的由来了："
