      "id": "rw-c4-p15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "num": "§ 15",
      "original": "Naturally, if that was her habit and that was her delight, she could only expect to be laughed at; and, accordingly, Pope or Gay is said to have satirised her \"as a blue-stocking with an itch for scribbling\". Also it is thought that she offended Gay by laughing at him. She said that his Trivia showed that \"he was more proper to walk before a chair than to ride in one\". But this is all \"dubious gossip\" and, says Mr. Murry, \"uninteresting\". But there I do not agree with him, for I should have liked to have had more even of dubious gossip so that I might have found out or made up some image of this melancholy lady, who loved wandering in the fields and thinking about unusual things and scorned, so rashly, so unwisely, \"the dull manage of a servile house\". But she became diffuse, Mr. Murry says. Her gift is all grown about with weeds and bound with briars. It had no chance of showing itself for the fine distinguished gift it was. And so, putting her back on the shelf, I turned to the other great lady, the Duchess whom Lamb loved, hare-brained, fantastical Margaret of Newcastle, her elder, but her contemporary. They were very different, but alike in this that both were noble and both childless, and both were married to the best of husbands. In both burnt the same passion for poetry and both are disfigured and deformed by the same causes. Open the Duchess and one finds the same outburst of rage, \"Women live like Bats or Owls, labour like Beasts, and die like Worms.... \" Margaret too might have been a poet; in our day all that activity would have turned a wheel of some sort. As it was, what could bind, tame or civilise for human use that wild, generous, untutored intelligence? It poured itself out, higgledy-piggledy, in torrents of rhyme and prose, poetry and philosophy which stand congealed in quartos and folios that nobody ever reads. She should have had a microscope put in her hand. She should have been taught to look at the stars and reason scientifically. Her wits were turned with solitude and freedom. No one checked her. No one taught her. The professors fawned on her. At Court they jeered at her. Sir Egerton Brydges complained of her coarseness—\"as flowing from a female of high rank brought up in the Courts\". She shut herself up at Welbeck alone.",
      "zh": "自然，倘若这就是她的习性，这就是她的乐事，她便只能等着受人嘲笑了；果不其然，据说[蒲柏或盖伊曾讽刺她是「一个犯了涂鸦瘾的女学究」]{#g-rw-c4-04}。此外据说她还曾因嘲笑盖伊而得罪了他。她曾说，盖伊那首《街头琐事》表明「他更配在轿子前头当开路脚夫，而不配坐在轿子里」。但这一切都只是「靠不住的闲言碎语」，照默里先生的说法，「无聊透顶」。不过在这一点上我可不敢苟同，因为我倒巴不得能多读到一些哪怕靠不住的闲言碎语，好让我能够查明或是拼凑出这位忧郁贵妇的几分面影——她热爱在田野间漫游，思索种种不寻常的事物，并且那样鲁莽、那样不智地蔑视「操持一所卑顺家宅的枯燥庶务」。可是默里先生说，她写得芜杂散漫起来。她的天赋四周杂草丛生，被荆棘死死缠缚。这本是一份细腻卓异的天赋，却根本没有机会显露真面目。于是我把她的诗集放回书架，转向另一位贵妇——兰姆所钟爱的那位公爵夫人，异想天开、疯疯癫癫的[纽卡斯尔公爵夫人玛格丽特]{#g-rw-c4-05}；她比温奇尔西夫人年长，却也是她的同时代人。她们两人截然不同，唯独在这几点上如出一辙：两人都出身贵族，都膝下无嗣，而且都嫁给了世上最好的丈夫。两人胸中都燃烧着同样炽热的诗歌激情，也都因同样的根由而面目扭曲、残缺不全。翻开这位公爵夫人的书卷，人们便会读到同样的狂怒爆发：「女人活得像蝙蝠或猫头鹰，劳作得像牲口，死得像蠕虫……」玛格丽特原本也有可能成为一位诗人；倘若放在我们这个时代，那股勃勃生机总能推动某只齿轮运转起来。可是在当时，有什么能够约束、驯化或陶冶那颗野性难驯、慷慨丰沛却未经调教的才智，使之有益于人间呢？它只是杂乱无章地倾泻而出，化作韵文与散文、诗歌与哲学的滚滚洪流，如今凝结在一卷卷无人问津的四开本与对开本巨册之中。当年本该往她手里塞一架显微镜才对。本该有人教她观测星辰、作科学的推理。由于孤寂与漫无拘束，她的神智走了岔路。没有人替她把关。没有人指点她。教授们对她曲意奉承。宫廷里的人对她冷嘲热讽。埃杰顿·布里奇斯爵士抱怨她文字粗鄙——「竟出自一位在宫廷里长大的高门贵妇之手」。她独自一人把自己关在韦尔贝克庄园里。",
